# SRG-APP-000825-WSR-000180  -  CAT II
# The web server must implement multifactor authentication for local;
# network; and/or remote access to privileged accounts; and/or nonprivileged
# accounts such that the device meets organization-defined strength of
# mechanism requirements.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000825-WSR-000180
group:           V-264344
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-004047
control_rev5:    IA-2

contribution:    For the web tier, Splunk expresses the multifactor mechanism the benchmark
                 authority names for this product (SPLK-CL-000490, a CAC or other PKI
                 credential) through web.conf [settings]: enableCertBasedUserAuth turns the
                 mechanism on, requireClientCert makes Splunk Web demand the token's
                 certificate, and certBasedUserAuthMethod binds it to an account. The
                 device's strength is organization-defined, so the criterion tests only
                 that the mechanism is configured.

governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] enableCertBasedUserAuth in true|True|TRUE|t|1|yes
check:           web.conf [settings] requireClientCert in true|True|TRUE|t|1|yes
check:           web.conf [settings] certBasedUserAuthMethod in CommonName|EDIPI|PIV
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] certBasedUserAuthMethod https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/authenticate-into-splunk-enterprise-using-hardware/configure-splunk-enterprise-to-use-a-common-access-card-for-authentication
vendor:          web.conf [settings] enableCertBasedUserAuth https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/authenticate-into-splunk-enterprise-using-hardware/configure-splunk-enterprise-to-use-a-common-access-card-for-authentication
vendor:          web.conf [settings] requireClientCert https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/authenticate-into-splunk-enterprise-using-hardware/configure-splunk-enterprise-to-use-a-common-access-card-for-authentication
# --- end vendor verification

external:        The strength-of-mechanism requirement itself - organization-defined; no
                 product setting measures a device's assurance level.
external:        The CAC/PIV token, its reader and middleware - carried by the DoD PKI and
                 the client operating system.
external:        certBasedUserAuthPivOidList is observed but not asserted: the spec
                 supplies a default OID list, so an unset value is not a shortfall even
                 when certBasedUserAuthMethod is PIV.
external:        Where the native Duo or RSA integration (authentication.conf
                 externalTwoFactorAuthVendor) or an approved PKI-terminating SSO proxy is
                 the configured path instead, the mechanism is carried there and these
                 web.conf settings may legitimately be absent.

citation:        splunk-spec-files/web.conf.spec:120, :133, :144
                 (certBasedUserAuthPivOidList default list), :151; SPLK-CL-000490 check and
                 fix text
finding:         Splunk Web does not require a multifactor device at login -
                 certificate-based user authentication is not enabled in web.conf
                 [settings] (enableCertBasedUserAuth, requireClientCert,
                 certBasedUserAuthMethod) and no vendor multifactor or approved SSO proxy
                 path was presented in its place; this determination is from the
                 perspective of the application layer alone, the device's strength being
                 organization-defined.
